     people usually mean by "notifications system," and it's the last one that can be built, not the first.

  Trying to spec 3 now would mean inventing 1 and 2 as assumptions and then almost certainly rewriting them.

  One thing I want to resolve before proposing where to start, because it changes whether notifications are meaningful
  at all: a static page with no server usually means one person on one browser. "Users get notified when tasks [1mthey[0m care
  about change" implies other people making changes you didn't make. If it's really single-user, most of a notification
  system collapses — you already know what changed, because you changed it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m multi-user is the difference between needing a server, identity, and per-user read state, versus needing
  none of that. It's the most expensive thing to retrofit and the cheapest thing to decide now.

  - [1mSingle-user, local[0m — no server, no accounts; [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is enough. "Notifications" become reminders (due dates,
    recurring) rather than social signals. Small, buildable this week.
  - [1mMulti-user, shared[0m — needs a backend, identity, persistence, and real-time or polled delivery. An order of magnitude
    more work, and it makes step 1 a server project rather than a page.
  - [1mSingle-user now, multi-user later[0m — build local, but keep the task model and event shape serializable so a server
    can slot in. Costs a little discipline now, avoids a rewrite.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the third, [3mif[0m multi-user is genuinely on the roadmap; otherwise the first. Building a backend for a
  notification system when there isn't yet a task to notify about is the expensive mistake here.
